Here is the Verilog code for the `TopModule` implementing the XOR gate in three different ways as requested.

### Logic Explanation
1.  **`out_assign`**: Uses a continuous assignment. This is purely combinational logic. The output changes immediately when inputs `a` or `b` change.
2.  **`out_always_comb`**: Uses a combinational `always` block. This is functionally identical to the assign statement but allows for more complex logic if needed. It is sensitive to all inputs (`*`).
3.  **`out_always_ff`**: Uses a clocked `always` block. This infers a Flip-Flop. The output is registered and only updates on the rising edge of the clock (`clk`). This introduces a one-cycle delay compared to the other two outputs.

### Verilog Code

```verilog
module TopModule (
  input clk,
  input a,
  input b,
  output out_assign,
  output reg out_always_comb,
  output reg out_always_ff
);

  // 1. Continuous Assignment (Combinational)
  // The output changes immediately when a or b changes.
  assign out_assign = a ^ b;

  // 2. Combinational Always Block
  // Functionally equivalent to the assign statement above.
  // The '*' means the block is sensitive to all signals on the right-hand side.
  always @(*) begin
    out_always_comb = a ^ b;
  end

  // 3. Clocked Always Block (Sequential)
  // This infers a flip-flop. The output is updated only on the rising edge of clk.
  // This means out_always_ff will be delayed by one clock cycle compared to the others.
  always @(posedge clk) begin
    out_always_ff = a ^ b;
  end

endmodule
```

